course: dictionary
lesson: 5207
title: One word, three jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: One word can do different jobs, and the context tells you which job it is doing. In «Japanese food», Japanese describes the food, so it is an adjective meaning from Japan.
explain: In «The Japanese are famous for their gardens», Japanese names a group of people, so it is a noun meaning the people of Japan. Always write it with a capital J.
explain: In «She can speak Japanese», Japanese names a language, the one written with kanji, hiragana and katakana. Ask yourself: is this word describing something, naming people, or naming a language? That question unlocks the meaning.
fact: Japanese is an adjective when it describes a thing, such as «Japanese garden».
fact: The Japanese are the people of Japan, an island country in East Asia.
fact: Japanese is also the name of the language of Japan.
fact: Japanese is always written with a capital J.
fact: «Kintsugi» is a Japanese art of mending broken pottery with gold.
exercise: «We ate Japanese food at the new restaurant.» What kind of word is Japanese here? | A noun for a group of people | *An adjective describing the food | A word for a language
exercise: «The Japanese welcomed us to their town.» Who does Japanese mean here? | The island of Mount Fuji | A type of pottery | Tourists from any country | *The people of Japan
exercise: «Jayden learned to speak Japanese at school.» What does Japanese mean here? | The people of Japan | A Japanese garden | A kind of art made with gold | *The language of Japan
test: «Hokusai, the Japanese artist of The Great Wave, made thousands of prints.» What does Japanese describe here? | The Great Wave as a people | *The artist, meaning he came from Japan | The prints as a language | The wave
test: Which sentence uses Japanese as a noun for a language? | Japanese culture is rich and old | The Japanese cooked lunch for everyone | Japanese gardens are peaceful places | *Tom is learning Japanese at school
exam: In «The Japanese Bon Odori honours the spirits of family members», what kind of word is Japanese? | A noun for a group of people | A noun for a language | A capital letter that starts a sentence | *An adjective, describing the dance
